<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warmadewa dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th–11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Warmadewa dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> for much of the 10th and 11th centuries, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-kesari-warmadewa">Sri Kesari Warmadewa</a>, named on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belanjong-pillar">Belanjong pillar</a> of 914. Its kings, including Udayana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anak-wungsu">Anak Wungsu</a>, ruled from the Pejeng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedulu">Bedulu</a> area and issued inscriptions in Old Balinese and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a>. Their marriage ties with the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> brought strong Javanese influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warmadewa-dynasty.jpg" alt="Warmadewa dynasty" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanur_Belankong_Pillar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
